  "tipUrgeSurfingTitle": "Surf the urge",
  "tipUrgeSurfingBody": "The urge usually peaks and fades on its own within 15 to 20 minutes. Instead of fighting it, just notice it without acting — \"urge surfing\" is a cognitive-behavioral technique backed by research for handling compulsions.",
  "tipHaltTitle": "Check HALT before you react",
  "tipHaltBody": "Hungry, Angry, Lonely, Tired (HALT) are the four states most linked to relapse in the prevention literature. Before judging the urge, ask whether one of these four is weighing on you right now.",
  "tipMapTriggersTitle": "Map your triggers",
  "tipMapTriggersBody": "Write down the time, place, and what you were feeling the last few times you nearly opened a risk app. Spotting the pattern ahead of time is one of CBT's core techniques for reducing relapse.",
  "tipTalkBeforeNeedingTitle": "Talk before you need to",
  "tipTalkBeforeNeedingBody": "Telling your trusted partner when the day gets hard, before any relapse, lifts the weight of shame and strengthens your support network — loneliness is one of the strongest triggers.",
  "tipPhoneOutOfRoomTitle": "Phone out of the bedroom at night",
  "tipPhoneOutOfRoomBody": "Moments of boredom and tiredness at night account for a good share of relapses. Charging your phone outside the bedroom removes easy access right at that time.",
  "tipRelapseNotFailureTitle": "A relapse doesn't erase your progress",
  "tipRelapseNotFailureBody": "The goal is the trend over time, not a perfect record. Treating a slip as total failure tends to make the relapse worse; refocus on the next moment instead of giving up.",
  "tipIfThenPlanTitle": "Have an if-then plan",
  "tipIfThenPlanBody": "Decide now: \"if I feel the urge to open [app], then I will [specific action]\". Research on if-then plans shows that pre-linking a trigger to a response works better than relying on willpower in the moment.",
  "tipMoveYourBodyTitle": "Move your body for 10 minutes",
  "tipMoveYourBodyBody": "Moderate-to-vigorous exercise reduces urge intensity almost immediately, according to studies across different types of compulsion. A brisk walk or a short workout already helps.",
  "tipSelfCompassionTitle": "Treat guilt with kindness, not punishment",
  "tipSelfCompassionBody": "Shame (\"I am the problem\") is linked to more relapse than self-compassion (\"I slipped, so what?\"). Acknowledging the slip without attacking yourself is what research links to a more stable recovery.",
  "tipWriteItDownTitle": "Write it down, not just in your head",
  "tipWriteItDownBody": "When an urge hits, note the situation, its intensity from 1 to 10, and what helped it pass. This structured log is a core CBT technique for revealing patterns memory alone misses.",

